  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nA mixed bag of conditions out there this afternoon. We have\npartial sunshine for most places with some haze from smoke\naloft. Around the International Border, we have had some\nthunderstorms as well. Storms have been fueled by strong warm\nair advection and quite a bit of instability in place through\nthe morning. There is also some cyclonic vorticity advection\naloft. Once storms formed, it looked like their movement was \nbased partially on where instability was and possibly storms \ndeveloping along their outflow boundaries. The Arrowhead saw a \nquick round of potentially severe storms with radar signatures \nsuggesting that there could have been some ~60 mph downburst \nwind gusts. \n\nFor this afternoon and evening, the main concern is more\npotential for thunderstorms. Vertical shear is expected to\ndecrease, and thus the threat for hail will likely be limited to\ninitial updrafts for short duration. Following that, storms may\nrain on themselves with the lack of shear, and then the primary\nthreat with any storms will be damaging downburst winds. The\npredictability of where and when storms may develop this\nafternoon is rather low, but the synoptic forcing is not\nexpected to change too much in placement, so northeast Minnesota\nwill see the highest chance for any severe storms, and that's\nwhere SPC maintains a marginal risk for severe storms. \n\nLow pressure will pass by to our south tonight, bringing plenty\nof rain to central/southern Minnesota, mainly along/south of\nI-94. Up here, rainfall amounts are expected to be less dramatic\nas a whole with less synoptic forcing and PWAT (though PWAT will\nactually still be high up here around 1.5-2.0\"). Widespread\namounts will likely hover around a quarter of an inch, but with\nPWATs being pretty high, any thunderstorms will have the\ncapability to produce locally high amounts over an inch. CAPE\nshould gradually decrease overnight, so the severe threat should\nbe limited to this evening. Flooding threat overnight is non-\nzero, but it's expected to be pretty isolated and only in any\nplaces where any persistent storms may develop (which isn't a\nguarantee). \n\nExpect rain to slowly move east on Friday as a cold front passes\nthrough. We will probably deal with lingering thunderstorms in\nnorthwest Wisconsin into the afternoon, and possibly along the\nNorth Shore. After the rain exits, high pressure is expected to\ndominate through the weekend. Temperatures will cool down a bit\nand we are expected to see some pleasant weather overall. Smoke\nconditions are likely to improve as well as we get more\nnortherly flow and less influence from wildfire smoke origins\nout west. \n\nFlow returns to south/southwesterly Sunday into Sunday night,\nand we should see warmer temperatures on Monday with highs in\nthe 80s. There's pretty good agreement on an upper level trough\nwith an attendant cold front swinging through around Monday\nnight. At this time, convective parameters as a whole don't look\ntoo impressive, but we'll have to keep an eye out for at least a\nsmall chance for a nocturnal squall line that could produce \nsome gusty winds. We will probably return again to more \nseasonable temperatures Tuesday into Wednesday with quieter \nweather, then maybe briefly warmer Thursday with another cold \nfront going into Friday.",
